<div id="2026-11-12-schema-compare-decimal-size" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-12 — Schema compare: decimal precision/scale now read from the Result file</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> <code>Comserv::Util::Schema::ResultParser</code> parses an array-ref attribute value (<code>size => [10, 2]</code>) instead of dropping it. <code>SchemaComparison::get_result_field_info</code> flattens that array to the DB parser's <code>"p,s"</code> string form, and the comparison loop no longer runs <code>0 + $size</code> on a reference.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Every <code>decimal</code> column in every Result file declares its precision as <code>size => [p, s]</code>, and the parser had no branch for a leading <code>[</code>: the value fell through to the bareword branch, which cannot match it, so <code>size</code> came back <code>undef</code>. The nested-hash branch had the same hole in another shape — it collected only <em>quoted</em> array items, so <code>[10, 2]</code> produced an empty list. Two consequences: the grid showed no size on the Result side (nothing to compare or edit for a column such as <code>inventory_items.net_weight_g</code>), and "add column to table" built the type from <code>data_type</code> alone, creating <code>decimal(10,0)</code> and silently discarding the scale. A sweep of all 294 Result files showed 218 of 219 decimal columns had no parsed size; all but a deliberately sizeless one now do.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Util/Schema/ResultParser.pm</code>, <code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Admin/SchemaComparison.pm</code>, <code>t/schema_result_parser_array_size.t</code> (new). Expect previously masked decimal size differences to appear in the grid once this loads — they are real drift, not new damage.</p>
</div>
